`rg -n '\"bad-signature\"' <reclaim/resign module>`). Ensure events with verdict `signer-not-on-keyring` are re-signed. Require that the full test passes, including its post-re-sign enforced-verify assertions, without weakening any of them.","cohort":["COH","E1","E2","E6","F1","T2","T4","T5b","T5e","T9"],"criteria":["E6","T2"],"decision":"dropped","decision_margin":-0.95,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["\"`717c50f8b33` ... added this test expecting the old `bad-signature` verdict for the fixture's off-keyring events. The two landed independently\"","The failure is at `tests/unit/test_reclaim_resign.py:382`, inside `test_resign_makes_enforced_verify_pass_with_era_keys`. The later re-sign/verify-pass assertions are unexercised.","The plan never mentions inspecting the re-sign code's failing-event selection for hardcoded verdict strings."],"finding":"The plan treats this as a stale test expectation only. However, the failing assert at line 382 sits before the test's real claim, that re-signing makes enforced verify pass with era keys. Every assertion after line 382 has never run against the new verdict vocabulary. Nothing in the plan checks whether the reclaim-collapse re-sign path from 717c50f8b33 selects events to re-sign by verdict. If it keys on `bad-signature` and/or `unsigned`, `signer-not-on-keyring` events would be skipped. Fixing line 382 could then expose a production defect, or prompt loosening further assertions.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f8e16a341c976396f","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Cause (from history) / Acceptance Criteria AC1","norm_id":"n14147856d0ced5e2","priority":0.0,"reason":"veto:cited-reference-inaccurate","recalled":false,"scenarios":["The re-sign logic filters on `verdict in {\"unsigned\", \"bad-signature\"}`. Off-keyring events are not re-signed, and the enforced verify still fails in production reclaim runs.","An executor updates line 382, hits a later assertion failure, and weakens that assertion to get green instead of fixing the re-sign selector."],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"Add an AC: \"The reclaim-collapse re-sign selector treats `signer-not-on-keyring` as a re-sign-eligible failing verdict (no hardcoded `bad-signature`-only match). The post-re-sign enforced-verify assertions in `test_resign_makes_enforced_verify_pass_with_era_keys` pass unmodified.\"","tier":"LLM","validity":0.2222,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"yes","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"no","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"no","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"no","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"no","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"no","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"no"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"none","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"none","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"self_revealing","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":0.95,"blocking_enabled":false,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Decide in the plan which verdict the test is meant to exercise (off-keyring era-key signers → `signer-not-on-keyring`) and drop or justify the forgery alternative so the fix keeps the test's scenario.","cohort":["E3","E5","F4","G5","T6","necessity","project.failure-disposition-contract","project.net-mechanism","project.portability"],"criteria":["E3"],"decision":"dropped","decision_margin":-0.675,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["\"added this test expecting the old `bad-signature` verdict for the fixture's off-keyring events\"","\"or the fixture producing a genuine forgery if `bad-signature` is what the test means to exercise\"","Test name: test_resign_makes_enforced_verify_pass_with_era_keys"],"finding":"The AC leaves the remedy as an open either/or: update the expectation to `signer-not-on-keyring`, or change the fixture to produce a genuine forgery. The plan never decides which one matches the test's intent. The test is named `test_resign_makes_enforced_verify_pass_with_era_keys`, and the plan describes the fixture's failing events as 'off-keyring events'. That suggests the test exists to show that re-signing repairs off-keyring (era-key) signatures. The forgery arm would quietly swap that scenario for a different one and drop coverage of the off-keyring re-sign path.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f020f83f166e70efe","impact":0.55,"is_primary":null,"location":"Acceptance Criteria, AC 1 ('...or the fixture producing a genuine forgery if `bad-signature` is what the test means to exercise')","norm_id":"ndf99a6dbe9279248","priority":0.275,"reason":"veto:absence-refuted","recalled":false,"scenarios":["The implementer picks the forgery arm because it is the smaller edit. The test then stops exercising re-signing of off-keyring era-key events, which is what 717c50f8b33 added it for, and that path goes untested."],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"Rewrite AC 1 to: 'Update the expected failing-verdict set to {\"unsigned\", \"signer-not-on-keyring\"}. Keep the off-keyring fixture so the test still proves re-signing repairs off-keyring era-key events.' Mention the forgery option only if the 717c50f8b33 author's intent is shown to be forgery repair.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.5,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"no","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"no","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"no"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"underspecified_oracle","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"low","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"none","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"silent","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"low"}}},{"block_threshold":0.95,"blocking_enabled":false,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Add a step to grep the reclaim/resign code from 717c50f8b33 (and any other verify-identity consumers) for `bad-signature` and update any verdict-based selection to include `signer-not-on-keyring`; require the test's post-resign enforced-verify assertion to pass unchanged.","cohort":["E3","E5","F4","G5","T6","necessity","project.failure-disposition-contract","project.net-mechanism","project.portability"],"criteria":["E3"],"decision":"dropped","decision_margin":-0.95,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["\"`9a6ed375112` ... changed `verify-identity` so a sound signature by a key that is not on the claimed author's keyring reports `signer-not-on-keyring` instead of `bad-signature`.\"","\"`717c50f8b33` ... added this test expecting the old `bad-signature` verdict\" — the same commit added the re-sign implementation, which landed independently of the vocabulary change.","Absence: no step audits non-test consumers of the verdict vocabulary in the reclaim/resign path."],"finding":"The plan treats the stale `bad-signature` string as a test-only problem. It does not check whether the re-sign logic added in 717c50f8b33 (\"Re-sign failing events during the reclaim collapse\") also selects events to re-sign by matching verdict strings such as `bad-signature`. If it does, then after 9a6ed375112 off-keyring events would no longer be picked for re-signing. In that case the production code needs updating as well as the test assertion. The plan acknowledges the verify-identity behavior change but not the downstream consumers that may depend on the old verdict.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f44d5272d5af5bdf2","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Cause (from history); Acceptance Criteria AC 1","norm_id":"nf0071598090b5f64","priority":0.0,"reason":"veto:cited-reference-inaccurate","recalled":false,"scenarios":["The reclaim collapse filters failing events on {'unsigned', 'bad-signature'}. Off-keyring events now report `signer-not-on-keyring`, are never re-signed, and enforced verify still fails after reclaim in real repositories. The test may still go green if only the assertion is edited and the later steps happen not to catch it."],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"","tier":"LLM","validity":0.2222,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"yes","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"no","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"no","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"no","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"no","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"no","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"no"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"none","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"none","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"self_revealing","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":0.8,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Pick one edit route in AC1. Either update only the expected verdict set at test_reclaim_resign.py:382, or, if the fixture changes, list every `_build`-dependent test (lines 378/415/452/471/485/506/528) that must still pass.","cohort":["G1G2"],"criteria":["G1G2"],"decision":"dropped","decision_margin":-0.734,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Plan AC1: 'with the failing-verdict expectation matching the current verify-identity vocabulary ... or the fixture producing a genuine forgery'","grep `_build(tmp_path)` in tests/unit/test_reclaim_resign.py: called at lines 378, 415, 452, 471, 485, 506, 528","test line 399: `assert result.events_resigned == 5  # X create + below, Y create + foreign + no-author`, which depends on the fixture's event mix"],"finding":"The acceptance criteria offer a second edit route ('or the fixture producing a genuine forgery') but do not say which files or tests it would touch. That route means changing the shared `_build` fixture in tests/unit/test_reclaim_resign.py, which 7 tests call (lines 378, 415, 452, 471, 485, 506, 528). Several of them depend on exact event counts, for example `result.events_resigned == 5`. The plan lists none of these affected tests, so the scope is unclear if the author picks this route.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"fb0d0015ee7867b25","impact":0.264,"is_primary":null,"location":"Acceptance Criteria, AC1 ('or the fixture producing a genuine forgery if `bad-signature` is what the test means to exercise')","norm_id":"nf76fb154448950fb","priority":0.066,"reason":"low-validity","recalled":false,"scenarios":["The author changes `_build` to make a forged signature, and the other 6 tests that share it start failing or silently test something different.","A reviewer cannot tell whether a diff that touches `_build` is in scope."],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"Make the primary fix a one-line change at tests/unit/test_reclaim_resign.py:382: `assert {f[\"verdict\"] for f in failing} == {\"unsigned\", \"signer-not-on-keyring\"}`. Drop the fixture-forgery alternative, or move it to a follow-up ticket if forgery coverage is wanted.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.25,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"yes","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"no","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"no","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"no","severity_claim_justified":"no"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"low","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"none","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"self_revealing","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"low"}}},{"block_threshold":0.8,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Commit AC1 to one approach: update tests/unit/test_reclaim_resign.py:382 to expect {\"unsigned\", \"signer-not-on-keyring\"}, keep the foreign-key fixture as it is, and add a short rationale (the fixture's foreign-signed events are sound off-keyring signatures, which is exactly the case reclaim_resign exists to re-sign).","cohort":["G6"],"criteria":["G6"],"decision":"dropped","decision_margin":-0.4562,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Plan AC1: 'with the failing-verdict expectation matching the current verify-identity vocabulary (`signer-not-on-keyring` for a sound off-keyring signature) or the fixture producing a genuine forgery if `bad-signature` is what the test means to exercise.'","src/rebar/_commands/reclaim_resign.py:4-6: 'Events that fail it (unsigned, or signed by a key that is not on the author identity's keyring) are re-signed during the rewrite'","tests/unit/test_reclaim_resign.py:149,189,264: a real 'foreign' keypair signs the folded and raw events with `_foreign_sig`, which gives a sound off-keyring signature","src/rebar/_commands/verify_authorship.py:156-157: a signature that matches no recorded key is `signer-not-on-keyring` when it verifies against its own embedded key","No positive rationale in the plan explains why either branch fits the test's intent"],"finding":"The AC offers two different fixes: (1) change the expectation to `signer-not-on-keyring`, or (2) make the fixture produce a real forgery so `bad-signature` stays. It never picks one or says why. Option 2 is clearly worse. reclaim_resign's stated purpose is to re-sign events 'signed by a key that is not on the author identity's keyring', and the fixture deliberately builds sound foreign-key signatures (`_foreign_sig`, 'foreign-signed raw', 'folded, foreign-signed'). Turning those into forgeries would remove the test's coverage of the off-keyring case the module exists to repair.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"fce9ad0594dfcf297","impact":0.55,"is_primary":null,"location":"Acceptance Criteria, AC1 ('…or the fixture producing a genuine forgery if `bad-signature` is what the test means to exercise')","norm_id":"n03e280183d23d6db","priority":0.3438,"reason":"veto:absence-refuted","recalled":false,"scenarios":["An implementer reads 'bad-signature is what the test means to exercise' literally, tampers with the foreign signature bytes, and the test goes green while it no longer checks that resign repairs off-keyring signers.","A reviewer sees a fixture-semantics change in a red-main hotfix and argues over it, which delays the unblock for every Gerrit change."],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"Replace AC1 with: '`pytest tests/unit/test_reclaim_resign.py -q` passes on main after line 382 is changed to `assert {f[\"verdict\"] for f in failing} == {\"unsigned\", \"signer-not-on-keyring\"}`. The fixture stays unchanged because its foreign-key events are deliberately sound off-keyring signatures, which is the case reclaim_resign re-signs (see reclaim_resign.py module docstring) and which verify-identity has reported as `signer-not-on-keyring` since 9a6ed375112.'","tier":"LLM","validity":0.625,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"no","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"no","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"yes"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"underspecified_oracle","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"low","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"none","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"silent","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"low"}}}],"impact_model_version":"plan-v5","material_fingerprint":"4e07d44e24691656","material_parts":{"children":["647ea2107ca5072f",0],"description":["2e772ab3afbe8676",1569],"description_boxes":["6c179f21e6f62b62",0],"file_impact":["a7fbe4617668482d",1],"ticket_id":["2b1b912fd285c9a1",19]},"metrics":{"cache_read_tokens":383954,"cache_write_tokens":133886,"claim_path":"no-llm/no-network (structural; the fast claim check is a local HMAC verify)","det_ms":13338.5,"input_tokens":540240,"llm_calls":28,"llm_ms":78384.8,"output_tokens":21416,"precheck":{"cited_anchor_warning":false},"total_ms":153731.9,"verify_requests":3},"model":"anthropic:claude-sonnet-5-5","priority_floor":0.8,"provider_provenance":{"candidates":["anthropic:claude-sonnet-5-5","anthropic:claude-sonnet-5-5"],"capabilities":{"native_structured_output":false,"prompt_cache_style":"anthropic","supports_temperature":false,"supports_thinking":true,"web_access":"off"},"endpoint_host":null,"model":"anthropic:claude-sonnet-5-5","provider":"anthropic","ran_model":"anthropic:claude-s